If you have anxiety, or even if you don't, parenting a tiny vulnerable baby is very intense. A baby's cry is very effective at getting a human's attention, gives me a nervous twitch just thinking about my kids at that age. A tip from our midwife, if you feel overwhelmed when your baby has been crying and crying, put the baby somewhere safe and step out of the room for a minute to collect yourself. Babies are very responsive to emotions of their carers so it is important to remain calm. 

&amp;#x200B;

Then there is the potential for being the autistic parent of an autistic child, and I have to say that my little buddy and I get along fantastically and it is very helpful knowing what he is going through, having been there myself. And having an autistic parent and an autistic child and autistic me being the link, lets just say that I'm a firm believer in autism having a genetic link.

"Isolation rooms" for UK's autistic children
Girl put into mainsteam school's "isolation room" for 240 days....for being autistic. Tell me again, Department of Education, how school is so vitally important that a child can't miss a day to attend a funeral of a non-immediate family member with the school's permission, or a week to go away with parents if they can't get time off in term-time, without paying a penalty of £160 but this girl and many others can be left to rot in a booth with no contact with other children and NO TEACHING. With parents not being told, even though the girl has selective mutism. And parents not being told of a letter the girl wrote saying she would harm herself.

&amp;#x200B;

I am so ashamed of our education system.

&amp;#x200B;

[https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/education-47898657](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/education-47898657)

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vju1EbVVgP8](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vju1EbVVgP8)

&amp;#x200B;

I recommend Amythest Schaber to everyone because she is autistic so speaks from experience, and her videos are very positive and informative.

This is good advice. Traditional methods of punishment (yelling, warning BAM consequence, things like that) can do more harm than good and lead to meltdowns rather than compliance. If he has PECS cards for communication, use them to show him what he is doing wrong and what you want him to do. Remember that he may need short, clear instructions with maybe only one task at a time (not "change into your pyjamas and brush your teeth" - that is 2 things) and again, a visual aid would help him if he has a chart at home that tells him what the schedule is. 

&amp;#x200B;

Will he be living with you full time, or does his mother have him too? If she is in the picture, she may be able to give advice and tell you how she does things. If the rules and schedules are the same if he is living between 2 houses, that will help somewhat.

Sounds really good. Do you include History in Humanities? Although it starts out pitched below your son's age level, we really like Story of the World as it naturally also dips into religions and covers so much. Also we haven't found a science curriculum that covers Everything quite the way Building Foundations of Scientific Understanding and again, although it starts out pitched at younger children, wow it is high level stuff.

&amp;#x200B;

There are so many of us homeschooling because public schools can't give our children the education that they deserve; you'll be in good company.

The great thing about getting older is the slow hearing loss. I'm not Old old but did have hearing loss after having a baby that cried very loudly in my ear for long periods of time. I've been better with noise since then but the downside is, my normal hearing is also worse so I really have to concentrate when talking to people with background noise. 

&amp;#x200B;

Can totally emphasise with noisy yard work. The worst is when they use leaf-blowers for EVERYTHING, blowing dirt off the drive and so on and it goes on forever. When did humans lose the ability to push a broom and use secateurs??! If Edward Scissorhands was remade today, he'd have a leafblower for one hand and hedge trimmers on the other.

Mine liked Math Seeds (same company as Reading Eggs) around that age. My autistic son hates pressure of any sort so online work is great for him. Some kids are like that, others seem to love proving themselves and don't mind tests and quizzes. 

&amp;#x200B;

Are you in the UK? American posters might not know that we start school aged 4 here, with children expected to be reading by age 5 (and preschool from age 2 or 3 before starting at 4).

We were in the same position with one of ours - she didn't cope with preschool at all and we could tell that she wasn't ready for school at all. I can't imagine all that many children are at 4! Is there a way you could do Irish at home, just incase a few years down the line she wants to go? Then she wouldn't be at a disadvantage. 

&amp;#x200B;

Socially, if you are on Facebook that seems to be the way of arranging things these days for Home Edders.

We homeschool and it is the best option for us. It only takes one unkind teacher to really destroy an autistic child's confidence, as we found out. A child going non-verbal is in such a high state of distress; to punish her is so very cruel. Her silence hurt no one. 

&amp;#x200B;

If you did have to work nights, do you have childcare so that you will actually get good quality sleep when you get home? A friend did this (nights + homeschooling) and gave up working after not too long as she was a zombie during the days. If you have a good support system in place, family or good childcare options, that will make it so much easier for you. Personally I would look for other options (weekends if you have a spouse not working then) before nights but if you are a hardy soul and can do OK on nights, anything is possible.

99% of all hermits were schooled traditionally. I made that statistic up, but it is likely somewhat truthful as the vast majority of children attend school. If you are able to share with them what social activities you have planned, that might help ease their worries. Afterschool sports, girl scouts, church groups, basically all the opportunities to chat and make friends with other kids.

&amp;#x200B;

They might change their minds after seeing how well it goes. Most of the homeschooled children we are around have great manners, are kind, and can converse quite happily with other children and adults.
